WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 70: The Great Mississippi Flood of 1927: The Deluge That Remade America

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.920
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.280
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:14.240
[Rebecca] In the spring of 1927, the mightiest river system in North America rebelled against human engineering.

4
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:28.320
[Rebecca] Following months of relentless autumn and winter rains across the entire heartland, the Mississippi River swelled into an inland sea, smashing through massive earthen levees in 145 catastrophic crevasses.

5
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:38.960
[Rebecca] 27,000 square miles, an area larger than the states of Massachusetts, Connecticut, and Vermont combined, were submerged beneath 30 feet of muddy, raging water.

6
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:52.240
[Rebecca] The catastrophe stranded hundreds of thousands on muddy levee crowns, accelerated the great migration to Chicago and Detroit, launched a future president, and forever transformed the soulful poetry of the Delta Blues.

7
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.960
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

8
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.640
[George] Championed by Brigadier General Andrew A.

9
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:29.520
[George] Humphreys, the doctrine dictated that by confining the mighty river within ever higher earthen walls without permitting natural floodways, wetlands, or spillway outlets, the river's rushing current would scour its own channel deeper into the riverbed, preventing catastrophic flooding.

10
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:44.960
[George] Driven by this hubristic conviction, states and local levee districts constructed hundreds of miles of towering earthen barricades, 30 feet high and hundreds of feet wide, walling off millions of acres of fertile floodplain forests for cotton plantations.

11
00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.520
[George] But the natural hydrology of a continent cannot be so easily chained.

12
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:57.520
[George] The Mississippi River drains 41% of the continental United States across 31 states and two Canadian provinces.

13
00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:03.000
[George] In the late summer of 1926, heavy rains began falling across the Midwest.

14
00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:14.280
[George] By winter, torrential downpours saturated the Ohio, Missouri, and Arkansas River valleys, sending staggering billions of gallons of runoff surging southward toward the Delta.

15
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.600
[George] Chief of Engineers General Andrew A.

16
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:29.800
[George] Humphreys insisted that natural river outlets and floodways were wasteful, enforcing a dogmatic policy that constricted the Mississippi into an artificial channel and caused water levels to rise higher with every spring flood.

17
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:38.440
[George] Local engineers and river pilots who had spent generations observing the seasonal currents pleaded with Congress to construct auxiliary channels.

18
00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:47.400
[George] Warning that confining the swollen river between ever higher earthen ramparts would only guarantee a catastrophic breach during an extreme rainfall year.

19
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:56.120
[George] By April 1927, the Lower Mississippi was a terrifying, swollen monster swollen to five times its normal volume.

20
00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:03.960
[George] The water pressed relentlessly against the waterlogged earthen embankments, boiling through saturated soil in dangerous sand boils.

21
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:14.920
[George] The catastrophic tipping point arrived on the stormy morning of April 21, 1927, at Mounds Landing, Mississippi, 12 miles north of Greenville.

22
00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:27.080
[George] Guarded by a crew of hundreds of conscripted African American laborers working in torrential rain under the rifles of local deputies, a forty-foot section of the earthen levee suddenly liquefied and collapsed.

23
00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:30.680
[George] In an instant, the river unleashed its cosmic fury.

24
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:36.920
[George] A wall of water 15 feet high roared through the gap with a deafening thunder heard 20 miles away.

25
00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:46.240
[George] Within hours, the crevasse widened to three-quarters of a mile across, discharging a volume of water more than double the entire flow of Niagara Falls.

26
00:03:46.480 --> 00:04:00.640
[George] The roaring deluge ripped mature cypress forests out by the roots, shattered plantation mansions, and submerged the rich cotton empire of the Yazoo Mississippi Delta beneath 10 to 30 feet of muddy water in less than 36 hours.

27
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:14.240
[George] The roar of the rushing water was heard for miles as 30-foot-high waves ripped mature cypress trees from their roots, tearing through cotton fields and engulfing tenant farm shacks before families could gather their belongings or livestock.

28
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:27.040
[George] Rescue skiffs navigating the swollen floodwaters encountered stranded families clinging to the tin roofs of submerged barns, surrounded by swirling brown water that stretched to the horizon like an inland sea.

29
00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:39.040
[George] As the floodwaters swallowed hundreds of towns across Mississippi, Arkansas and Louisiana, the only dry land remaining in the entire delta was the narrow, muddy crowns of surviving levees.

30
00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:56.080
[George] In Greenville, Mississippi, over 13,000 refugees, the overwhelming majority poor African American sharecroppers and tenant farm families, huddled on the eight-foot-wide levee embankment for weeks, exposed to cold rains, swarms of mosquitoes and hunger.

31
00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:02.080
[George] The racial caste system of the Jim Crow South manifested in cruel and devastating relief.

32
00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:16.400
[George] Wealthy white plantation owners, terrified that their cheap agricultural labor force would escape northward, deployed armed National Guardsmen to patrol the perimeter, refusing to allow black families to board evacuation steamboats.

33
00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:35.800
[George] While white women and children were evacuated to safety in Vicksburg and Memphis, black refugees were confined to segregated levee camps, forced at gunpoint to perform hazardous relief labor, and made to stand in separate lines for Red Cross emergency rations, sparking outrage across the national black press.

34
00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:48.760
[George] Delta planters feared that if African American sharecroppers evacuated on rescue steamers, they would never return to work the cotton fields, leading local authorities to enforce armed curfews on the muddy levee crown.

35
00:05:48.760 --> 00:06:00.600
[George] National Guard units armed with rifles patrolled the Levee Crown, strictly forbidding black laborers from boarding evacuation barges bound for Memphis or Vicksburg under threat of physical violence.

36
00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:06.200
[George] Two hundred miles to the south, the city of New Orleans was gripped by blind terror.

37
00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:18.920
[George] Surrounded by water on three sides and resting below sea level, the historic French quarter and downtown banking district were threatened with total submersion if the swelling river overtopped the municipal flood walls.

38
00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:25.400
[George] In late April, an elite cabal of New Orleans bankers and political oligarchs met in secret.

39
00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:37.240
[George] They pressured federal authorities and Louisiana Governor Orimil Simpson to take an unprecedented, desperate step, deliberately blast opened the levee 13 miles downriver at Carnarvon in St.

40
00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:41.960
[George] Bernard Parish, sacrificing the poor farming and trapping communities of St.

41
00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:48.040
[George] Bernard and Plaquamines parishes to divert water and save the commercial wealth of the metropolis.

42
00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:58.600
[George] On April 29, before thousands of spectators and national newsreel cameras, military engineers detonated 39 tons of dynamite along the Carnarvon Levee.

43
00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:06.760
[George] The explosion shattered the earthen wall, flooding thousands of homes and trapping marshlands of innocent Cajun and Ailenio families.

44
00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:19.200
[George] In a bitter irony of geography, subsequent engineering studies revealed that natural crevasses upstream had already lowered the river level, rendering the destruction of Carnarvon completely unnecessary.

45
00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:19.600
[George] Sent.

46
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:34.400
[George] Bernard and Plaquamines parish trappers and farmers watched in horror as their homes, muskrat marshes and orange groves were inundated to protect the financial district of New Orleans, harboring bitter resentments that lasted for generations.

47
00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:46.960
[George] The decision to sacrifice the rural downstream parishes generated fierce outrage among local Creole fishermen and smallholders, who stood weeping on the levee as 60 tons of dynamite shattered the earthen wall.

48
00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:51.120
[George] The catastrophe demanded unprecedented federal mobilization.

49
00:07:51.120 --> 00:08:00.560
[George] President Calvin Coolidge appointed his ambitious, energetic Secretary of Commerce, Herbert Hoover, to lead the special Mississippi Flood Relief Commission.

50
00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:10.720
[George] Hoover, who had earned an international reputation as the master organizer of humanitarian relief in war torn Europe, seized the opportunity with administrative brilliance.

51
00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:28.400
[George] Hoover organized a fleet of more than 800 commercial vessels, steamboats, and Coast Guard cutters, established 154 tent cities, and directed the distribution of millions of dollars in Red Cross medical supplies, vaccines, and emergency food rations.

52
00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:38.960
[George] Hoover's face was plastered across newspaper front pages and newsreel theaters for six consecutive months, hailed as the master of emergencies who saved a million lives.

53
00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:51.600
[George] The national gratitude and administrative aura generated by his flood relief leadership became the primary political springboard that propelled Herbert Hoover into the White House in the presidential election of 1928.

54
00:08:51.600 --> 00:09:06.600
[George] Hoover mobilized the American Red Cross and commercial radio networks in an unprecedented national relief effort, delivering regular radio addresses that burnished his reputation as the great humanitarian and propelled him to the presidency in 1928.

55
00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:20.680
[George] Hoover coordinated 1,500 rescue boats, 60 naval seaplanes, and hundreds of refugee tent cities along high railroad embankments, demonstrating an unprecedented capacity for federal disaster logistics.

56
00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:28.840
[George] Beyond politics, the Great Flood of 1927 permanently altered the cultural and demographic map of the United States.

57
00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:38.520
[George] Betrayed by plantation owners who had forced them into labor at gunpoint on the levees, hundreds of thousands of African American sharecroppers made a fateful decision.

58
00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:41.160
[George] They would never return to the cotton fields.

59
00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:48.200
[George] In the months following the flood, a monumental exodus swept the South, accelerating the Great Migration.

60
00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:58.200
[George] Entire communities boarded northbound Illinois central passenger trains, carrying their families and their music to the industrial promise of Chicago, Detroit, St.

61
00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:00.280
[George] Louis and Cleveland.

62
00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:03.880
[George] The tragedy gave birth to a profound artistic flowering.

63
00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:09.640
[George] Delta Blues pioneers who survived the catastrophe channeled their grief into timeless recordings.

64
00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:20.120
[George] Charlie Patton's haunting, High Water Everywhere, Bessie Smith's Backwater Blues, and Memphis Minis When the Levy Breaks, later immortalized by Led Zeppelin.

65
00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:26.680
[George] The raw pain and resilience of the refugee camps were transformed into the bedrock of modern American music.

66
00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:43.320
[George] Blues legends like Charlie Patton, Sun House, and Bessie Smith channeled the terror and dislocation of the disaster into haunting songs, chronicling the high water, forced labor, and mass migration that permanently transformed American music and northern cities.

67
00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:59.680
[George] The Great Migration accelerated dramatically in the wake of the flood, as hundreds of thousands of African American families boarded Illinois Central Trains heading north toward Chicago, Detroit, and Cleveland, carrying their southern blues traditions to industrial centers.

68
00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:05.840
[George] The disaster fundamentally broke the Army Corps of Engineers failed levees-only dogma forever.

69
00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:18.640
[George] In May 1928, Congress passed the Landmark Flood Control Act of 1928, authorizing the largest civil works project in human history, the Mississippi River and Tributaries Project.

70
00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:23.920
[George] Engineering policy was completely revolutionized under Major General Edgar Jadwin.

71
00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:46.480
[George] Abandoning the illusion that the river could be wholly contained within walls, engineers constructed a multi-layered system, raising levees, establishing massive designated floodways like the Bonnet Carey Spillway and the Morganza Spillway that could divert excess floodwaters harmlessly into Lake Ponchartrain and the Achafalia Basin and preserving natural wetlands.

72
00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:53.120
[George] Today, the project has protected the lower Mississippi Valley from repeat disasters for nearly a century.

73
00:11:53.120 --> 00:12:08.000
[George] The Great Flood of 1927 remains an unforgettable American milestone, an epic testament to the folly of human hubris, the immense power of nature, and the resilient courage of those who rose from the deep waters to reshape a continent.

74
00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:23.440
[George] The monumental engineering legislation mandated the construction of the Morganza and Bonnet Carey spillways, acknowledging that the mighty Mississippi could never be fully contained by dirt walls alone, but required natural floodways to release its destructive fury.

75
00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:37.480
[George] The flood permanently transformed the relationship between the federal government and local communities, establishing the precedent that major natural catastrophes required federal financial responsibility and comprehensive regional planning.

76
00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:48.760
[George] The enduring lessons of the 1927 deluge reshaped American civil engineering, disaster relief protocols, and federal infrastructure policy for the modern era.

77
00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:57.160
[Rebecca] The Great Mississippi Flood of 1927 taught America that we cannot conquer nature, but must learn to live within its laws.

78
00:12:57.160 --> 00:12:58.520
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

79
00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:05.880
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey along the dark, forgotten forest highway of the Deep South, the Natchez Trace.

